WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.819
Some super interesting primary results from
yesterday, progressive activist Cory Bush

00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:11.480
has defeated 10 term U.S. Congressman Lacy
Clay in the Democratic primary in the first

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.380
district in Missouri.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:18.860
And this is an interesting seat because it
has actually been in the Clay family since

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:20.360
the 1960s.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.410
Bill Clay represented the district for 30
years.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:30.040
Then his son, William Lacy Clay Junior, was
elected in 2001.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:36.239
So it's been since the 1960s that this seat
has been in the same family in Missouri.

00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:43.059
Now, back in 2018, Corrie Bush ran against
Lacy Clay and lost by 20 points.

00:00:43.059 --> 00:00:46.730
Not a small defeat there for Cory Bush.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:51.239
Last night, she won roughly 51 to 49 percent.

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:53.210
As of the latest numbers I saw.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:56.980
Corey Bush is a nurse and an activist.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:04.070
And this is a pretty similar situation to
AOSIS District in New York City, where the

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:09.990
winner of the Democratic primary is overwhelmingly
likely to win the general in November.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:14.420
So essentially, the Democratic primary is
is really the big election.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.000
There is a Republican candidate.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.170
It's Anthony Rogers.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:21.430
He won the Republican primary last night as
well.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:26.189
And it is you might be if you're not familiar
with the Missouri first district, you might

00:01:26.189 --> 00:01:30.210
be surprised to hear that it is such a safe
district for four Democrats.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.320
It's an extremely urban district.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:38.409
So as we know in the United States, there
are voting differences among religions, their

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:41.750
voting differences among age, voting, differences
among race.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:44.799
But the urban rural divide is also significant.

00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:50.070
And this is a very urban district and widely
expected to go to Quarrie Bush in a similar

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:54.600
situation as that which we saw with AOC not
long ago.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:02:01.240
Now, there is an important reminder here about
how you we can't really evaluate the potential

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.340
impact of a Biden presidency without taking
into consideration the House and Senate.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:09.870
Now, we've talked about the Senate before
and with the Senate, it's a much more obvious

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:16.140
analysis, which is if Republicans can continue
to control the Senate during Joe Biden's at

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:22.000
least first two years in office, if Biden
were to win, there is going to be a dramatically

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.830
smaller number of even potential big progressive
victories.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:31.200
If you have Biden in the Oval Office, a Democratically
controlled House and a Republican Senate.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.540
It's going to be a fight.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:33.780
It's going to be obstruction.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.200
And the amount that can get done is going
to be much more limited.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.120
If Democrats can take the Senate and keep
the House.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.700
And Joe Biden wins, you will have a two year
period during which you could see some pretty

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:48.490
major progressive advancements made.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:49.610
They might not be perfect.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:54.650
They might not be what Bernie would do, but
they would be significant and notable advancements

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:59.010
in the progressive, potentially more progressive
house.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:03.080
The effect is more nuanced because what we're
talking about in the House is it's already

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.370
democratically controlled.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:09.110
Every expectation is that Democrats will retain
control in the House of Representatives, but

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.470
will it move to the left?

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:18.120
And when Cory Bush replaces Lacy Lacy Clay,
that is a potential move to the left for sure.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.470
And that is a more nuanced situation.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:27.830
Will the bills that make it to Biden's desk,
which he will be forced to sign or not sign?

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:33.490
And of course, that would be after conferencing
with the Senate, assuming Democrats take it

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:35.740
a more progressive House.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:41.090
Also better positions us to put more progressive
legislation in front of Joe Biden, potentially

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:42.320
getting him to sign it.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.430
So this is a really big deal.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:51.820
And if we do see these two years of total
control by Democrats, White House, Senate,

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.190
House, we're very much getting ahead of ourselves.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:56.670
We're not going to put the cart before the
horse here.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:03.350
You could see one of the greatest reversals
in a two year period from the completely whacked

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:08.960
and outrageous White House of Donald Trump
with a Republican Senate that has gotten so

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.510
many judges in, appointed for and now confirmed
for life positions.

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:17.890
You could actually see a really big reversal
to American politics.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.630
Would it be what Bernie would do if he was
given carte blanche?

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:22.019
No, but that's not the system we have.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:23.570
We have a system where we have a house.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.210
We have a Senate.

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:26.620
We have states rights.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:27.620
We have a White House.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.790
And it would be a very, very interesting situation.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:31.810
Now, as far as Corey Bush.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:34.010
She supports Medicare for all.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:38.770
She supports a green new deal, supports 15
dollar minimum wage, wants public funding

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.480
of elections and takes no corporate PAC money.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:47.870
So a very interesting replacement to the Kirt,
still incumbent as far far as the House is

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:49.310
concerned.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:55.930
Lacy Clay, this also this is maybe one other
angle worth touching on.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:59.610
This does suggest that AOC was not an anomaly.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:00.610
Or an anomaly?

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:06.880
Both, of course, valid pronunciations, and
that this may be a real wave of replacing

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:13.640
some of these more entrenched legacy Democrats
with younger, more progressive alternatives,

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:19.780
the effect that they will have, much like
in 2010 when the Tea Party surfaced and won

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:24.710
a bunch of seats just because they were winning
seats, didn't tell us what effect they would

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:27.630
have on the national politics or on the Republican
Party.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:32.450
We later saw that they certainly became a
notable voice in the Republican Party and

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:33.860
was sort of absorbed into it.

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:35.030
Now there's a Tea Party caucus.

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:41.970
It's still an open question what the longer
term effect is of these progressive victories

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:42.970
in the House.

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:47.560
And much of it will depend again on the circumstances
of who's in the White House starting in January

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.050
and who controls the Senate starting in January.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:54.690
And whether you do or don't like any individual
policy from any of these progressive replacements

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:59.590
to legacy Democrats, that's less important
than the realization that there is a changing

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:03.919
of the guard happening here, though I don't
think that can be denied anymore.

00:06:03.919 --> 00:06:09.020
The open question that we will have to answer
over time is what will the effect be on not

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:11.890
just the ideology of the country, but on the
policy of the country as well?

